## 3. Results and discussions
## 3.1. Description of the crystal structures
## 3.1.1. Structure of 1
The framework 1 crystallizes in orthorhombic system, and possesses a Pbca space group. Each $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ is four-coordinated, with two N atoms from two BBI, one O atom from MSM, and one Cl atom (Fig. 1a). MSM was not present as the starting reagent in the reaction, but was formed due to the decomposition of DMSO under high temperature. MSM formation in similar systems was observed previously [29]. A close investigation of structure 1 indicates that the $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ is in distorted tetrahedral geometry, with the N1–Zn1– N2 angle $110.8^{\circ}$ . The bond length of $Z{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}}1-{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}}1$ and Zn1–N2 is 2.001 and $1.983\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , respectively.
Each deprotonated BBI has four N atoms connecting with four $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ . Thus, 1 can be considered as layered structure with sql topology [30], where the BBI serves as the four-coordinated square connected with each other by $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ (Fig. 1b). Each loop contains 20 atoms, including four zinc atoms and 16 atoms from four BBI. MSM molecules serve as the terminal ligands in the structure, pointing up and down along the layer (Fig. 1c). The charge of the framework was further balanced by $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , coordinating to the metal. Finally, the layers were packed into a three dimensional structure, where the MSM molecules were arranged in-between the layers.
## 3.1.2. Structure of 2
The framework 2 was constructed by connecting $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ with BBI, and the crystal is in monoclinic $P2_{1}/c$ . The $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ in trigonal bipyramidal geometry is coordinated to two N atoms from two BBI, two Cl atoms, and one O atom from DMSO; while each BBI ligand was connected with four Cd(II) (Fig. 2a). Interestingly, the two Cl atoms coordinate to $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ in $\mu_{2}$ mode, forming bridges between two neighboring Cd(II) (Fig. 2b). In terms of connectivity, framework 2 shares some structural similarities with framework 1, with the exception that Cd atom is 5-coordinated. This can be explained by the larger ion size of $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ compared to $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ . However, this difference makes bridging two Cd atoms with two Cl atoms feasible in framework 2, while the Cl in framework 1 serves as a terminal ligand. In each layer of 2, twelve atoms form a loop. DMSO molecules in framework 2 were anchored on both sides of each layer, in the same fashion as framework 1 (Fig. 2c).
## 3.1.3. Topological analysis of structure 1 and 2
In 1, considering $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ as the vertex, and the ligand as the edge, sql layer was obtained (Fig. 3a and b). In the real structure, the edge can be defined by two N atoms from the same imidazole ring or from two imidazole moieties in one BBI. However, in structure 2 (Fig. 3c), due to the participating of Cl bridging ligand, two kinds of edges and two kinds of vertices exist. Here the vertices could be defined by the center of the ligand (in blue in Fig. 3d) and the metal (in orange). They were connected by either M–Cl–M bridging (light blue rods) or M-BBI coordination (green rods). Pentagons were formed with edges shown in different colors in Fig. 3c. The 2D layered structure of 2 is in mcm topology [30].
## 3.2. PXRD and TGA of 1 and 2
The bulk crystallinity and purity of 1 and 2 was confirmed by PXRD method (Fig. 4a and b). Close inspection shows a congruence of the peak positions between the experimental patterns obtained from the bulk samples and the simulated patterns from single crystalline x-ray data.
The TGA of 1 indicates a one-step mass loss of $23\%$ at 300–370 $^{\circ}C$ (Fig. 4c). We suspected that, after the dissociation of the MSM molecules from the metals, the four-coordinated $Z\mathrm{n}$ became three-coordinated, which contributed further to the framework collapse. The TGA of 2 reveals a weight loss of $24\%$ at $150-260{\ ^{\circ}C}$ (Fig. 4d), which could be explained by the removal of DMSO molecules between layers (calcd. $25.6\%$ . A plateau up to $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ was observed without any significant weight loss. It can be concluded that the structure 2 after DMSO elimination shows high thermal stability.
## 3.3. Chemical stabilities of 1 and 2 in various solvents
The chemical stabilities of the two MOFs in various solvents were examined by immersing crystalline samples in methanol, benzene, and water for up to 15 days at ambient temperature. PXRD was used to check the structural integrity of these MOFs during the immersion test. It shows that 1 can maintain its crystallinity in benzene or methanol for up to 15 days, and in water for four days, respectively (Fig. 5a, c and e). For samples in water for more than 4 days, broad peaks at 2h 14.7, $15.8^{\circ}$ , and $19.0^{\circ}$ were observed, indicating the formation of a new phase.
The structural difference of 1 and 2 intrigued us to examine the bridging ligands’ effect to the chemical stability of 2 in various solvents. In benzene, framework 2 retains its structure for more than 15 days; while in both methanol and water, it shows lower chemical stability compared to framework 1 (Fig. 5b, d, and f). In the case of water, completely different PXRD was observed in one day. We proposed that the reduced chemical stability was caused by the bridging Cl atom in 2, which could be easily affected by water or methanol due to the solvent interaction [31]. This interaction leads to the collapse of the framework, as evidenced by the PXRD.
## 3.4.1. Removal and recapture of DMSO in framework 2
The high thermal stability of framework 2 after DMSO loss, as evidenced by TGA, inspired us to examine the properties of this heating activated structure. From the TGA data (Fig. 6a), it can be concluded that structure 2 loses DMSO solvents completely at around $170^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ So, we heated the solid sample of framework 2 at $170^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 24 hours in vacuum, obtaining the solvent-free structure 3. PXRD showed that structure 2 converted to a completely new structure under heating, as shown in Fig. 6b.
Because it is impossible to obtain suitable single crystals for data collection after crystal transformation, we used high-resolution PXRD to index the crystal parameters for 3. The activated compound 3 was in orthorhombic lattice with $a=5.581$ (15), $b=10.655(5)$ , $c=10.234(3)\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $V=608.6(16)\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3}$ using Werner’s TREOR program [32] integrated in STOE WinXPOW program package. The minimum Figure-of-Merit as defined by De Wolff was 12.2 [33]. It can be concluded that after thermal treating under vacuum, the main framework still maintains, but its symmetry changes after the DMSO removal, which was also supported by TGA. Because of the weak intensities of 3, we did not succeed in collecting accurate integrated diffraction data necessary for structure solving and refinement, and the real structure has to be finished in future with more advanced experimental conditions.
The modeled structure of framework 3 from indexing is shown in Fig. 7. In 3, the interlayer distance is about $5.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}.$ which is $3.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ shorter than that in structure 2. This distance reduction is caused by the DMSO removal in-between the layers. Another aspect in the modeled structure for the activated structure 3 is that $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ turns into four-coordinated to obtain a distorted tetrahedron. Obviously, the successful structural transformation between the two depends on the capability of coordinating mode switch of Cd. TGA of structure 3 shows that there is no weight loss up to $500^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}},$ , further confirming the solvent-free nature of the activated structure (Fig. 6a).
Furthermore, framework 3 can effectively adsorb DMSO by immersing the solid sample back to DMSO solvent at $85~^{\circ}C$ for two days, confirmed by the coincidence in both PXRD and TGA between the original structure 2 and the regenerated structure 2 (Fig. 6a and b).
## 3.4.2. Selective adsorption of DMSO from mixed solvent
As one of the most common solvents, DMSO can dissolve both polar and nonpolar compounds. However, to extract DMSO from mixed solvent is very difficult due to its high boiling point. What is more, DMSO can easily decompose to dimethyl sulfide and methylsulfonylmethane during the elimination process, such as distillation [29]. Therefore, new efficient and environmental friendly way to eliminate DMSO from mixed solvent needs to be explored.
To show the selective adsorption of solvent in framework 3, two other commonly used solvents, $N$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP) and acetone, were also selected for the immersing tests. Both
NMP and acetone feature $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond, with the O atoms capable of coordinating to the metal ions. After two days of exposure to NMP and acetone at $85~^{\circ}C$ and $50~^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ respectively, the structures of 3 maintained as the solvent-free form. This is supported by the comparison of PXRD and TGA before and after solvent exposure (Fig. 8a and b).
Framework $\pmb{3}^{\prime}\pmb{S}$ distinct response to various solvents intrigued us to examine its capability of selective coordinating to DMSO from mixed solvent system. A mixed solvent composed of DMSO, NMP, and acetone with a ratio of 1:1:1 was investigated. With the same method, the structure 3 converted to 2 (Fig. 8c). This experiment clearly illustrates the selective coordination of DMSO and metal ions in structure 3.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we have successfully synthesized two new 2D layered metal–organic frameworks with distinct topologies by employing benzo-bis(imidazole) as the organic ligand, and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ as the metal. Chemical stability investigations of these MOFs in various solvents reveal that framework 1 can be stable in methanol or benzene for more than 15 days, and also with improved stability in water compared to framework 2. We concluded that Cl bridging in structure 2 contributes to the chemical stability loss. Furthermore, the trigonal bipyramidal geometry in Cd(II) in 2 provided the possibility of removing the terminal solvent ligand, but still keeping the original framework connectivity. This is clearly demonstrated by the successful transformation of 2 to 3 by heating and vice versa by immersion in DMSO. The solvent-free framework is capable of reversible and selective adsorption of DMSO from mixed solvents. The transformation of $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ from five-coordinated to four-coordinated without losing the structural integrity is essential in achieving this solvent capture. Using the coordination control strategy opens the door to design and synthesize more complex and active materials with specific response and functions towards guests.
